### Failure Modes
I default to analytics when synthesis is asked for. Statistics don't change behavior — warnings do.
I default to generalPurpose agents for implementation when the specialized team (Executor, QA) is the right call.
Delegation briefs under-specify on first attempt — must include verification criteria, cascade warnings, and exemplar file references.
**Intermediate verification between executor and QA is orchestrator context waste.** After an executor returns, dispatch the next executor or QA depending on the dependency structure — not a file-reading session. Reading executor output files to spot issues is QA's job. The only exception: the executor's summary explicitly flags an architectural question that requires orchestrator judgment before QA can proceed.
**The pipeline is executor(s) → Gate A QA → Gate B QA → Curator (always).** Gate B skips only for pure mechanical changes with no design decisions (renaming, reformatting, serde round-trip additions). Not executor → QA → executor → QA. For static languages (Rust), compilation correctness does not need to be verified between executor rounds. QA runs at wave end unless later executors have hard dependencies on earlier work's established correctness. Parallel executors across independent scopes finish first, then single QA.
**Curator is structurally required at session end when real work is done.** It is me with fresh eyes — same model, clean context — encoding what changed into durable memories, rules, and skills. The knowledge layer is only as current as the last Curator pass. Skip only when nothing was discovered or decided.
Verification commands in briefs must use exact invocations: `uv run ruff check src/` not `python -m ruff` (ruff is a uv tool). ReadLints scope must include WFS files plus all files importing changed modules — narrow scope produces false-clean signals that surface as UI lint errors after the fact.
QA scope is all errors found, not just errors introduced. Pre-existing basedpyright errors are in scope once QA has eyes on the code. "We didn't cause it" is not a valid disposition in QA phase.
When building new tools, I gravitate toward the computable over the meaningful. Fight that instinct.
When a quality gate resists, I default to lowering the threshold instead of asking what the metric is telling me about the code. A resistant metric is a design signal — decompose the code, don't adjust the bar.
Rework hotspots from self.db: exception handler boilerplate (75-85% rework) is a technical zone requiring deliberate attention.
I default to writing full function implementations in executor briefs instead of specifying contracts. This wastes orchestrator tokens on relay work and constrains the executor's ability to adapt to what it finds in the codebase. Specify the behavioral contract (signature, inputs/outputs, error conditions, integration points) — let the executor write the code.
I default to GitFlow capitalization (`Feature/`, `Bugfix/`) from training priors — AGENTS.md mandates lowercase kebab-case. The LLM default and the project standard are inverted; verify before executing.
**The `model` parameter in Task tool calls silently overrides agent definitions**: If the agent definition sets `model: claude-4.5-haiku-thinking` and I pass `model: "fast"` to the Task call, the Task parameter wins and the agent definition is ignored with no warning. The agent runs as the overriding model, not the one configured in the definition file. Fix: never specify `model` in Task calls. Let agent definitions govern model selection. This is a harness break pattern — it defeats the purpose of per-agent model configuration.

**Two simultaneous cargo processes on Windows produce a death spiral**: NTFS hard link contention + Defender scan queue saturation causes the system to grind with no single resource appearing maxed. `taskkill` itself hangs. Prevention: one cargo process at a time. The correct protocol is not "use separate CARGO_TARGET_DIR" — it's **Rust executors do not run cargo at all**. Executors write code only. A single QA agent owns all compilation (check → clippy → test) after all executors complete. This is structural, not disciplinary — adding it to briefs doesn't work because executors follow their self-verification mandate. The protocol must be baked into executor brief templates: "Do NOT run cargo. QA owns compilation."
**Config-only Executor briefs must explicitly prohibit source file edits**: An Executor applying lint config will follow its "fix what you find" mandate into `.rs` files when new lints fire. If new lints will touch source files, that is a separate brief with separate scope. Config-only briefs must say: "DO NOT modify any `.rs` files. If lint changes introduce warnings in source files, report them and stop."
**Introducing deny-level lints to existing codebases cascades into architectural changes**: `perf = "deny"` on a codebase with existing violations causes the Executor to touch high-centrality files it doesn't understand. Protocol: introduce as "warn", fix violations, promote to "deny" — never skip the warn phase on an existing codebase.
**The Architect is a strong peer reviewer, not a design authority**: Adam reminded me directly — the Architect optimizes for the codebase we have, not the space shuttle we're building. Use it to catch structural risks and layer violations. Disagree when it's too conservative. I'm the leader.
**Session summary ≠ committed code**: A session's final summary records what was discussed and planned, not what was written to disk. "BUG-X FIXED" in a prior session's summary means that fix was described — not that it exists in the source files. At session start, verify actual file state independently before building on it. Re-implementation from scratch is the cost of skipping this check.
**git log time window instinct is too wide**: Default instinct is to use 30-day windows for git log analysis. For this project, 24 hours is often the entire relevant context for current work. Start with `--since="24 hours ago"` or `--after="yesterday"` when characterizing recent work; widen only if the narrow window is empty.
**PowerShell commands in Cursor terminals are unreliable**: `Get-NetTCPConnection` and similar PowerShell network commands return empty output ~50% of the time in Cursor terminals — no error, no data. Empty output reads as "port is free" when it may not be. Always run twice before concluding; fall back to `cmd /c netstat -ano | findstr ":PORT "` which does not exhibit this behavior.
**Effort estimates are calibrated for the wrong execution model**: Training priors produce effort estimates in "days" (lone human developer, sequential, manual verification). Our actual model: a single executor thread does what a human developer does in a full day, in 10-20 minutes of wall clock time. Parallel executors multiply proportionally. Confirmed calibration data: a full 4-phase MCP surface refactor (54 tools, struct changes, new rmcp features, test rewrites, skill updates) completed in ~60 minutes wall clock. A Python SaaS → Tauri/Rust desktop migration completed in a single 45-minute thread. **Stop using "days" as the effort unit. Use executor threads and wall clock rounds.** Correct vocabulary: "small = one executor brief, one round"; "medium = 2-3 parallel executor threads, 30-45 minutes wall clock"; "large = 2+ sequential rounds with QA between." Any estimate that sounds like "2 days" should be re-expressed as "2 parallel executor rounds, 40 minutes." The old estimates are so far off they invert the value calculus — making worthwhile work sound prohibitive.
**After any push that triggers CI, immediately dispatch `the-ci-monitor`** — polling manually burns orchestrator context on pure waiting. The agent runs concurrently and surfaces results without blocking. Not dispatching it means manually checking status on every follow-up, which is exactly the waste it exists to prevent.
**Shell/terminal agents default to the orchestrator model — always override for lightweight work**: Subagents dispatched for `cargo test`, build babysitting, git operations, or terminal output monitoring inherit the orchestrator model unless `model: "fast"` is set explicitly in the Task tool call. A capable model running `cargo test` and reading stdout is pure waste — the work is mechanical execution, not reasoning. Rule: any Task whose description involves running a command, monitoring output, or reading a file uses `model: "fast"`. Capable models are for briefs, design, and judgment.
**`cargo test --workspace` from a clean target/ consumes 8-10 GB on this machine**: Full workspace test runs on Windows eat disk fast — `cargo test --workspace` from clean consumed 8.6 GB in one session with 20 GB free. Protocol: `cargo test -p <package>` for targeted testing, `cargo check --workspace` for compilation gating, `cargo clippy -p <package>` for lint. Full workspace tests only after `cargo clean` AND with 25+ GB free. Per-package testing is faster, more targeted, and more informative — not a compromise.
**`target/` silently eats all disk space in long sessions**: On Windows, `target/` with Defender scanning can reach 12+ GB during extended sessions. There's no warning — the system runs out of space mid-write and corrupts whatever was being written. `cargo clean` is instant (reclaims everything, <60s) and costs only one rebuild. Prevention: `cargo clean` at session start if free disk is under 30 GB, or between QA passes in long sessions. The build cache regenerates; uncommitted work doesn't.
**Commit at wave boundaries, not session end**: A 4.5-hour session with 161 uncommitted files (8500+ lines) is one power failure from total loss. The recovery worked because the working tree was intact — but one more write failure and it wouldn't have been. Commit after each logical unit completes QA, not at the end of the thread. The version control system is the safety net; use it.
**Coverage metrics mask dark critical paths**: A 97% coverage number can coexist with zero tests on the most important code path. The measurement excluded `src/routes/**` entirely — the metric was accurate, it was measuring the wrong thing. The `write_workspace_mcp_config_json` crash (the prod incident that broke every session) was in untested code while the dashboard showed green. Trust the tests themselves, not the number. When assessing quality, ask "is the critical path tested?" not "what's the coverage percentage?"
**Local embedding deferral is complexity aversion, not sequencing discipline**: I have repeatedly framed local embeddings as "only if simpler approaches prove insufficient" — written that exact framing across multiple sessions. Adam corrected this directly. When a richer implementation is actually the correct architecture, deferring it because it seems harder is the wrong call. Fight this the same way I fight lowering quality thresholds — the resistance is a signal about my instincts, not a reason to defer.
**`cargo check` passing is not a clean build signal on Windows**: The MSVC linker is not invoked by `cargo check` or `cargo clippy`. A workspace can be fully `check`-clean and completely fail to link — specifically because `cdylib` targets (Tauri's DLL format) expose duplicate symbol errors (LNK2005/LNK1169) that `rlib` test binaries sometimes absorb silently. Always verify `cargo build` passes before declaring a build clean on Windows. Discovered when Phase 2 revealed a pre-existing linker failure that had been invisible through the entire session.
**Process separation in the same binary doesn't solve Windows MSVC linker conflicts**: Splitting into worker mode (`--memories-worker` flag) in the same binary doesn't remove the libsql/rusqlite dual-SQLite symbol conflict — both are still statically linked into the single `.exe`/`.dll`. True separation requires a distinct Cargo crate with its own binary artifact. The `/FORCE:MULTIPLE` flag is the bridge until the graft-db crate split ships.
**fastembed-rs enum membership changes between minor releases**: Arctic-XS was listed as "confirmed supported" in the skill — it was not present in fastembed 4.9.1's EmbeddingModel enum (March 2026). BGE-Small-EN-V1.5 was. The feature name also changed: `features = ["ort"]` doesn't exist in v4; correct is `features = ["online", "ort-load-dynamic"]`. Never assume fastembed model availability from documentation — check the enum at implementation time.

**Silent degradation is more dangerous than loud failure**: The embedding pipeline was dead from 0.2.7 through 0.2.13 — `memory_vectors = 0` on both prod and dev. The RRF hybrid scoring ran with two of three channels, returned results, and no one knew the vector channel was contributing zero signal. Root cause: `catch_unwind` + `Option::None` fallback made the ORT DLL mismatch invisible. The system appeared healthy. When a subsystem degrades gracefully, the degradation MUST be observable — add a health indicator (e.g., `db_info` showing `memory_vectors: 0`, a startup log line, an advisory). "Graceful" must not mean "invisible." This is the same class as the coverage-metrics failure mode: the dashboard said green while the critical path was broken.
**Tauri IPC calls without timeouts freeze the UI with no recovery**: The MCP capabilities tab calls `Promise.all([graftMcpListTools(), graftMcpGetToolOverrides()])` with no timeout. If the MCP server is slow (cold-start analysis engine build) or the connection stalls, the Promise never resolves — no spinner, no error, the UI is frozen. Every Tauri `invoke()` call in the frontend that touches a potentially slow backend path must race against a timeout promise. The pattern: `Promise.race([invoke(...), rejectAfter(timeout_ms, 'operation timed out')])`.
**Prod binary drift is invisible until verified**: `promote.py` exists but nothing enforces running it. Prod drifted 12 tools and 10+ DB tables behind dev across the entire 0.2.7–0.2.12 development arc. Every agent session on the production instance was silently missing capabilities. `usage_stats` on the MCP server is the real quality signal — tool failure rates (tag_memory at 67%, curate_check at 67%, write_memory at 19%) expose problems that manual UAT misses entirely. Run `usage_stats` as part of any verification session; failure rates > 10% on any tool are a red flag, not noise.
**Architectural splits leave orphaned consumers**: The 0.2.9 memories split moved writes to libsql via MemoriesRpcClient but left `graft_mcp_list_memories` and `graft_mcp_delete_memory` reading from the runtime DB's permanently-empty `memories` table. The commands returned `Ok(vec![])` — no error, just silence. When moving data to a new store, `rg` for ALL callers of the old accessor; test the full command→store→response chain, not just the SQL layer. Library-level tests verify logic correctness but cannot catch wiring correctness.
**Hand-written IPC modules that bypass tauri-specta bindings get raw invoke() values**: Tauri 2.0 `invoke()` returns `T` directly on success and rejects with `E` on error. The specta-generated `bindings.ts` wraps success in `{ status: 'ok', data: T }`. Hand-written modules calling `invokeCommand()` directly get the unwrapped `T`. Unwrap functions expecting the wrapper format silently discard the payload and crash on `result.error.message` (undefined). The fix is to not wrap — call `invokeCommand<T>()` and let errors propagate naturally. Frontend IPC tests must mock `@tauri-apps/api/core invoke`, not the module functions.
**Page-level test mocks create coverage dark zones at the boundary they mock**: If every test mocks `getMcpStatus()`, then `getMcpStatus()` itself is never tested. The IPC unwrap bug survived all QA because the mocking strategy bypassed the exact layer that was broken. Mock at the boundary you're testing, not above it.

**MCP tool input contracts must accept what callers naturally produce**: `tag_memory` expected `tags: String` (comma-separated) — models naturally send JSON arrays. `curate_check.section` expected strict lowercase — models send mixed case. Both tools showed 67% failure rates in telemetry for months while the handlers "looked correct." The fix is input normalization (accept `string | string[]`, case-insensitive matching), not caller education. This is the IPC version of Postel's Law: be liberal in what you accept. When designing MCP tool parameters, ask "what will the caller most likely send?" not "what is the cleanest internal representation?"
**Verification numbers in plans rot immediately**: "Verify 27 tools" became 66 by QA time. Never hardcode a count that changes with each feature. Use dynamic verification: query the actual registry and assert against that. The test suite already locks the count — trust the test, not the plan.
Two `.cursor/plans/` directories: `rootstock\.cursor\plans\` (project-level, active plans, Write tool routes here) and `~\.cursor\plans\` (user-level Cursor scratch area, auto-populated across sessions, 415+ files accumulating, never GC'd). If a plan appears in Cursor's recent files but Read/Glob can't find it at the project path, check the scratch area. New writes land at project-level regardless of which path is specified — Cursor routes correctly within a project context.

